 In Adobe JavaScript, you can force a text field to display columns using the `textFlow` and `ParagraphFormat` properties. Here's an example of how to create a text field with two columns:

1. **Basic Example**: To create a text field with two columns, you can use the `textFlow` and `ParagraphFormat` properties.
   ```javascript
   var textField = app.activeDocument.textFrames.add({geometricBounds: [100, 100, 400, 300]});
   var textFlow = textField.textRange.textFlowProperties;
   textFlow.columns = 2;
   textFlow.columnWidth = 200;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.alignment = TextAlignment.JUSTIFY;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.leftIndent = 10;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.rightIndent = 10;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.lineSpacing = 12;
   textField.contents = "Column 1\nColumn 2";
   ```

   This will create a text field with two columns, each column 200 pixels wide, and justified text with 10 pixels of indentation on both sides and a line spacing of 12 pixels.

2. **Using `columnWidth`**: The `columnWidth` property determines the width of each column. For example, if you want to split the text into two columns with a width of 250 pixels each, you can do it like this:
   ```javascript
   var textField = app.activeDocument.textFrames.add({geometricBounds: [100, 100, 400, 300]});
   var textFlow = textField.textRange.textFlowProperties;
   textFlow.columns = 2;
   textFlow.columnWidth = 250;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.alignment = TextAlignment.JUSTIFY;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.leftIndent = 10;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.rightIndent = 10;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.lineSpacing = 12;
   textField.contents = "Column 1\nColumn 2";
   ```

3. **Using `columnCount` and `columnWidth`**: If you need to change the number of columns, you can use the `columnCount` property. For example, if you want to split the text into three columns with a width of 150 pixels each, you can do it like this:
   ```javascript
   var textField = app.activeDocument.textFrames.add({geometricBounds: [100, 100, 400, 300]});
   var textFlow = textField.textRange.textFlowProperties;
   textFlow.columns = 3;
   textFlow.columnWidth = 150;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.alignment = TextAlignment.JUSTIFY;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.leftIndent = 10;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.rightIndent = 10;
   textField.appliedParagraphFormat.lineSpacing = 12;
   textField.contents = "Column 1\nColumn 2\nColumn 3";
   ```

In this example, the text is split into three columns, each 150 pixels wide.

Remember to adjust the `geometricBounds` property of the text frame to fit the desired size of the text field on your document.

This script will create a text field with the specified number of columns and column width, and fill it with the provided text. You can modify the text, column count, and column width to suit your needs.

